    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Relationships between Iranian parents and adult children combine emotional attachment, practical assistance, expectations, and disagreement. Tehran-based research shows why living arrangements, contact, and relationship quality must be considered separately when assessing support in later life.",
    "tags": ["Intergenerational relationships", "Older adults", "Family support", "Caregiving", "Social connection"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:22:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:22:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Intergenerational Relationships in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand parent–adult child relationships in Iran through research on practical support, emotional closeness, family expectations, and ambivalence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "dimensions-of-family-ties",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than a generation gap",
        "content": "Intergenerational relationships are the ties connecting family members across generations. For older parents and adult children, these ties include affection, contact, practical assistance, shared values, and expectations about family obligations. The intergenerational solidarity framework treats these as distinct dimensions rather than assuming that a family is simply close or distant. [s1]\n\nThat distinction is useful in interpreting Iranian family life. Agreement about obligations does not necessarily mean agreement about daily decisions. Frequent contact need not eliminate tension, and helping a relative does not reveal everything about emotional closeness. Research on the Persian version of an intergenerational relationship scale therefore considers conflict alongside solidarity. [s1]\n\nA further concept is ambivalence: attachment and strain can coexist in the same relationship. The framework describes tensions between providing support and maintaining independence, potentially experienced by both parents and children. It offers a way to understand mixed feelings without automatically treating them as evidence that family relationships have failed. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "support-and-households",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Support extends beyond the household",
        "content": "A Tehran survey distinguished support actually received from support a person believes would be available when needed. Received instrumental support concerns practical assistance; perceived support concerns the person's sense of access to supportive relationships. Measuring one does not substitute for measuring the other. [s2]\n\nIn that study, family size, living arrangements, and contact with relatives were associated with receipt of practical support. Living arrangements and relationship quality were associated with perceived support. The association between family size and perceived support also differed between women and men. These findings caution against assuming that simply having more relatives guarantees the same experience for everyone. [s2]\n\nThe study's definition of family included relatives living elsewhere as well as household members. This matters when reading household statistics: separate addresses do not, by themselves, establish the absence of a family connection. Equally, sharing an address is insufficient evidence of a supportive relationship. Contact, assistance, and relationship quality require their own questions. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coexisting-solidarity-and-conflict",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Closeness and disagreement can coexist",
        "content": "A separate analysis of older Tehran residents examined solidarity, conflict, and ambivalence in relationships with adult children. Its findings included evidence of both solidarity and tension. The important interpretive point is that support and disagreement are not mutually exclusive descriptions of a family tie. [s3]\n\nThe study interviewed parents without obtaining corresponding interviews from their children. It therefore documents the older generation's perspective rather than establishing how both sides understood the same relationship. Its urban sample also cannot describe rural families or every regional community in Iran. [s3]\n\nThese limits matter for claims about social change. A cross-sectional survey records relationships at a particular time; it cannot by itself prove that successive generations are becoming progressively less caring. The strongest reading of the available evidence is more specific: family connections have several dimensions, and understanding later-life support requires attention to the quality and circumstances of those connections. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
